    "why": "Neonatal calves are born with specialized, primitive <b>fetal-type enterocytes</b> capable of non-specific pinocytosis, taking up large macromolecules intact into cytoplasmic vacuoles and exocytosing them into lymphatic lacteals.<br><br>Furthermore, neonatal abomasal gastric acid and pancreatic trypsin secretion are delayed, protecting colostral immunoglobulins from degradation. Within 24–36 hours, these fetal enterocytes are sterically displaced by mature, microvillar absorptive enterocytes lacking pinocytotic uptake ('gut closure').",
    "clinical": "Administering colostrum to a 48-hour-old calf provides localized gut mucosal protection, but zero systemic IgG absorption occurs, failing to correct systemic Failure of Passive Transfer."
