    "comparison": "Equine Foals vs Bovine Calves",
    "why": "Equine red blood cells possess highly immunogenic, polymorphic surface antigens, notably <b>Aa and Ua</b>. When an Aa-negative mare is bred to an Aa-positive stallion, transplacental microbleeds or previous foaling immunize the mare against the Aa antigen, concentrating high-titer anti-Aa alloantibodies into her colostrum.<br><br>Upon nursing, the foal absorbs these antibodies, triggering massive extravascular and intravascular haemolysis. In cattle, bovine blood group antigens have low immunogenicity, and cows rarely produce high-titer colostral alloantibodies against their calves.",
    "clinical": "An apparently healthy newborn Thoroughbred foal suddenly develops severe weakness, jaundice, tachycardia, and dark red urine (haemoglobinuria) within 12–48 hours of first suckling; immediate removal from the dam and transfusion of washed mare RBCs is required."
