    "why": "The primary 'shock organ' in anaphylaxis depends on the anatomical distribution of smooth muscle and the concentration of mast cells and histamine/leukotriene receptors in the vasculature.<br><br>In dogs, the shock organ is the <b>hepatic veins and splanchnic circulation</b>; hepatic venous sphincter constriction causes massive venous pooling in the liver and gastrointestinal tract, leading to severe intestinal congestion and collapse. In cattle and sheep, the shock organ is the <b>pulmonary vasculature</b>, where severe bronchospasm and pulmonary arterial constriction lead to acute asphyxiation and pulmonary emphysema.",
    "clinical": "Anaphylactic dogs present with violent vomiting, bloody diarrhea, and sudden hypovolemic shock; anaphylactic cattle present with sudden open-mouth gasping, frothy nasal discharge, and cyanosis."
