    "id": 66,
    "title": "Why do Staphylococcal enterotoxins trigger massive cytokine storms, while conventional bacterial antigens activate fewer than 0.01% of T-cells?",
    "category": "species",
    "unit": "unit-4",
    "comparison": "Superantigens vs Conventional Antigens",
    "why": "Conventional antigens must be processed into 8–15 amino acid peptides by antigen-presenting cells and slotted into the peptide-binding groove of MHC molecules to be recognized by a specific T-cell receptor (TCR).<br><br>Superantigens (e.g., Staphylococcal enterotoxin B, TSST-1) bypass processing entirely: they bind directly to the <b>outer lateral surface of MHC Class II</b> and cross-link it to the <b>variable beta (Vβ) domain</b> of the TCR. This non-specific physical cross-linking activates all T-cells bearing that Vβ family, triggering a massive, lethal cytokine storm (IL-1, IL-2, TNF-α).",
    "clinical": "Staphylococcal toxic shock produces peracute fever, widespread erythematous rash, sudden hypotension, and multiorgan failure in susceptible animals without requiring prior antigen sensitization."
